% ============================================================================
% planet-ordinal-position — each planet's position from the Sun as an ORDINAL
% WORD ("third", not just "3"), DERIVED (not looked up) by bridging an
% astronomy fact to a math fact (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY, cross-directory
% composition with MATHEMATICS). Grounds the common early-elementary framing
% "Earth is the THIRD planet from the Sun."
% ============================================================================
% `astronomy/planets.adj` already tables each planet's order from the Sun as a
% NUMBER ("earth" is position 3); `mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj` already
% tables each NUMBER's ordinal WORD (3 is "third"). Neither file states
% outright "Earth is the third planet" — but composing the two ALREADY-SHIPPED
% facts answers it directly. `planets.adj`'s own header already notes its
% `order` column "composes into arithmetic"; this is the SAME kind of
% composition, just into ordinal LANGUAGE instead of arithmetic. This is the
% same "compose two independently-citable facts into a derived, dual-cited
% conclusion" discipline `language/word-families.adj`,
% `physics/heat-causes-phase-change.adj`, and
% `earth-science/season-start-month-number.adj` already established — the
% SECOND genuinely CROSS-DIRECTORY `rule` composition in this library
% (astronomy + mathematics, not two files in the same subject directory).
%
%     import "planet-ordinal-position.adj"
%     ? planet_ordinal_position(earth, $O)     % third
%     ? planet_ordinal_position($P, eighth)    % neptune (reverse recall)
%     ? planet_ordinal_position(pluto, $O)     % abstain -- not one of the 8 major planets
%
% This is a native `rule` (ADJ-TABLES RS-6): its body binds `$Planet`/`$Number`
% through the already-shipped `planet_order` table, then re-binds
% `$Number`/`$Ordinal` through the already-shipped `ordinal_number` table -- a
% plain two-hop join (the same shape `season-start-month-number.adj` already
% established), not a causal inference; it just names the standard convention
% that a counting number has a corresponding ordinal word. A query's `steps`
% trail shows THREE entries: the rule's own citation (the ordinal-word
% convention), the `planet_order` fact's citation (NASA), and the
% `ordinal_number` fact's citation, so a human can independently verify each
% link. Honest abstention on any body `planets.adj` does not table -- Pluto
% (reclassified a dwarf planet in 2006) is deliberately NOT a row in the
% source table, so this rule abstains on it too, rather than inventing a
% position.
%
% CROSS-DIRECTORY IMPORT: this file lives in `astronomy/` (its natural
% subject home, alongside `planets.adj`) and imports its mathematics sibling
% with a relative `../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj` path -- the SAME shape
% `earth-science/season-start-month-number.adj` already established and
% empirically verified (in a scratch dir, before writing this file, following
% that precedent exactly): the CLI's import sandbox is rooted at the
% TOP-LEVEL PROGRAM's own directory, not each importer's directory, so this
% resolves cleanly provided the consuming entry program lives at or above the
% package root. This library's OWN `.query.adj` companion is therefore placed
% at the package root (not beside this file), mirroring
% `mathematics/word-problems.query.adj` and
% `season-start-month-number.query.adj`'s already-established pattern.
%
% Deliberately scoped to the STANDARD ordinal-word bridge only, over the
% canonical eight major planets `planets.adj` already tables -- no new planet
% or ordinal-number content is asserted here, only the join. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "planets.adj"
import "../mathematics/ordinal-numbers.adj"

rule { head: planet_ordinal_position($Planet, $Ordinal)
       when: planet_order($Planet, $Number), ordinal_number($Number, $Ordinal)
       source "1 one first, 2 two second, 3 three third, 4 four fourth, 5 five fifth"
       locator "https://www.ef.edu/english-resources/english-grammar/numbers-english/"
       trust consensus }
